2.7.m:
because the confluence of all sacred pilgrimage places is in the hand of a brahmin. / From brahmins arose the three worlds, with the moving and the unmoving.
Their curses are proclaimed in the three worlds to be infallible, O knower of dharma; / and the blessing of brahmins is declared to be infallible.
Therefore, the best of the twice-born should be made intent on blessing by the king; / by whom fire was made all-devouring and the great ocean undrinkable,
by whom Soma was made to wane, and Śatakratu given the testicles of a goat, / and the fire of whose anger even today does not subside in Daṇḍaka.
And their favor is vast, O son of Bhṛgu; / by whose favor the moon, even when waned, longs for waxing;
by whose favor the sun is not overcome by the rākṣasas; / by whose favor Kārtavīrya attained vast fortune;
by whose favor Dhanada attained the superintendence of wealth; / the unbathed obtains the merit of bathing, and the vowless obtains a vow;

Worship of Keśava, O Rāma, is nevertheless fruitful for a woman;
Govinda is [the indweller of] all beings, the [true] husband, and none other for a woman.
Therefore, even without the husband's permission, His worship is prescribed.
